  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "source": "# 2.01 — The Erlangen Program\n\nIn 1872, at the age of 23, Felix Klein took up a chair at Erlangen and set out a programme for what geometry *is*. Stage 1 arrived at his answer by accident, one notebook at a time; here we state it deliberately.\n\n> **Klein's thesis.** A geometry is a set $X$ together with a group $G$ acting on it. The propositions of that geometry are exactly the statements invariant under $G$.\n\nThree consequences follow immediately, and they are what make the idea more than a slogan.\n\n**Geometry becomes plural.** There is no such thing as *the* geometry of the plane. Choose $E(2)$ and you get Euclidean geometry; choose $\\mathrm{Aff}(2)$ and lengths cease to exist but ratios survive; choose $PGL(3)$ and even parallelism dies. Same set, different geometries, because different groups.\n\n**\"Congruent\" is defined by the group.** Two figures are the same iff some $g \\in G$ carries one to the other — that is, iff they lie in the same $G$-orbit. All triangles are congruent affinely; only some are congruent Euclideanly. The word \"same\" has no meaning until the group is named.\n\n**Geometries are ordered by inclusion.** If $H \\le G$ then $H$-geometry is *finer* than $G$-geometry: fewer motions, so fewer figures identified, so more invariants and more theorems. The lattice of subgroups of $PGL(3)$ is a lattice of geometries.\n\nThis notebook sets up the vocabulary — action, orbit, stabiliser, invariant, homogeneous space — and then does the one computation that makes the framework concrete: identifying each geometry with a quotient $G/H$.",
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "source": "## 1. The vocabulary\n\nLet a group $G$ act on a set $X$, meaning each $g \\in G$ gives a bijection $x \\mapsto g\\cdot x$ with $e\\cdot x = x$ and $(gh)\\cdot x = g\\cdot(h\\cdot x)$.\n\n| Term | Definition | Geometric reading |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Orbit** $Gx$ | $\\{g \\cdot x : g \\in G\\}$ | everything congruent to $x$ |\n| **Stabiliser** $G_x$ | $\\{g : g\\cdot x = x\\}$ | the symmetries fixing $x$ |\n| **Transitive** | one orbit only | the space looks the same everywhere |\n| **Invariant** | $f(g\\cdot x) = f(x)$ | a legitimate geometric quantity |\n| **Homogeneous space** | $X$ with $G$ transitive | a geometry in Klein's sense |\n\nTwo facts do most of the work.\n\n**Orbit–stabiliser.** The map $gG_x \\mapsto g\\cdot x$ is a bijection $G/G_x \\to Gx$. When the action is transitive this says\n\n$$X \\;\\cong\\; G/G_x,$$\n\nso **a homogeneous space is a quotient of its own symmetry group**. Choosing a base point turns the geometry into group theory. (We already used the finite version of this in 1.04, where $|O_i| = N/n_i$ counted pole orbits.)\n\n**Invariants separate orbits.** A function is $G$-invariant precisely when it is constant on orbits, so invariants are exactly the functions on the orbit space $X/G$. \"Find the invariants\" and \"classify the figures up to congruence\" are the same problem, and 1.07 solved it for $E(n)$: the orbit space of $k$-point configurations is coordinatised by the pairwise distances.\n\nThe pattern to hold on to: **transitive on points, and then the stabiliser is what remains.** For $E(n)$ acting on $\\mathbb R^n$, translations make it transitive and the stabiliser of the origin is $O(n)$, so\n\n$$\\mathbb R^n \\cong E(n)/O(n).$$",
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "source": "load(\"numerics\")$\nload(\"ax-plots\")$\nload(\"linearalgebra\")$\nratprint : false$\n\ncvec(v) := transpose(matrix(v))$\npt(M)   := args(transpose(M))[1]$\ntsimp(e) := if matrixp(e) then matrixmap(lambda([z], trigrat(z)), e)\n            else if listp(e) then map(lambda([z], trigrat(z)), e)\n            else trigrat(e)$\n\n/* --- Orbits and stabilisers, computed for a finite group action --- */\n/* D4 acting on the plane: the orbit structure IS the geometry.\n   (Note: the helper is named orb, not orbit — orbit is a Maxima builtin.) */\nRot(θ) := matrix([cos(θ), -sin(θ)], [sin(θ), cos(θ)])$\nRef(α) := matrix([cos(2*α), sin(2*α)], [sin(2*α), -cos(2*α)])$\n\nclose_group(gens, maxsize) := block([G, frontier, newones, g, h, p],\n  G : [ident(2)], frontier : G,\n  while length(frontier) > 0 and length(G) <= maxsize do (\n    newones : [],\n    for g in frontier do for h in gens do (\n      p : tsimp(g . h),\n      if not member(p, G) and not member(p, newones) then newones : cons(p, newones)),\n    G : append(G, newones), frontier : newones),\n  G )$\n\nD4 : close_group([Rot(π/2), Ref(0)], 50)$\nprint(\"|D4| = \", length(D4))$\n\norb(G, v)       := unique( map(lambda([M], tsimp(pt(M . cvec(v)))), G) )$\nstabiliser(G, v):= sublist( G, lambda([M], tsimp(pt(M . cvec(v))) = v) )$\n\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"  point        |orbit|   |stabiliser|   product\")$\nfor v in [ [1,1], [1,0], [0,0], [2,1] ] do block([O, S],\n  O : orb(D4, v), S : stabiliser(D4, v),\n  print(\"  \", v, \"        \", length(O), \"          \", length(S),\n        \"           \", length(O)*length(S)) )$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"orbit–stabiliser: |orbit| · |stabiliser| = |G| = \", length(D4), \" every time.\")$\nprint(\"The origin is fixed by everything (orbit 1); a generic point has\")$\nprint(\"trivial stabiliser and a free orbit of size |G|.\")$",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": 2,
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "|D4| =  8\n  point        |orbit|   |stabiliser|   product\n   [1,1]          4            2             8\n   [1,0]          4            2             8\n   [0,0]          1            8             8\n   [2,1]          8            1             8\norbit–stabiliser: |orbit| · |stabiliser| = |G| =  8  every time.\nThe origin is fixed by everything (orbit 1); a generic point has\ntrivial stabiliser and a free orbit of size |G|."
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "source": "## 2. Geometries as quotients $G/H$\n\nThe orbit–stabiliser bijection above is not merely a counting device. It says that a homogeneous space *is* a coset space, and that gives a uniform description of every geometry in this journey.\n\nPick a base point $o \\in X$ and let $H = G_o$ be its stabiliser. Then $X \\cong G/H$, and the pair $(G, H)$ contains all the information. Here is the dictionary:\n\n| Space $X$ | Group $G$ | Stabiliser $H$ | $\\dim G - \\dim H$ |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| $\\mathbb R^n$ (Euclidean) | $E(n)$ | $O(n)$ | $\\binom{n+1}{2} - \\binom n2 = n$ |\n| $\\mathbb R^n$ (affine) | $\\mathrm{Aff}(n)$ | $GL_n(\\mathbb R)$ | $n^2 + n - n^2 = n$ |\n| $S^n$ (spherical) | $O(n+1)$ | $O(n)$ | $n$ |\n| $\\mathbb{RP}^n$ (projective) | $PGL_{n+1}(\\mathbb R)$ | stabiliser of a point | $n$ |\n| $\\mathbb H^n$ (hyperbolic) | $O^+(n,1)$ | $O(n)$ | $n$ |\n\nRead the sphere row carefully, because it is the template for Stage 2 and for all of Stage 5:\n\n$$S^n \\cong O(n+1)/O(n).$$\n\nA point of the sphere is a coset — the set of rotations carrying the north pole to that point. They differ by a rotation *about* the pole, and that ambiguity is exactly $O(n)$. Nothing is lost: the sphere has been rebuilt out of its own symmetry group.\n\nThe last column is the check that these are genuinely $n$-dimensional. It also exposes something: for a fixed $X$, a *larger* $G$ forces a larger $H$. Euclidean and affine geometry describe the same $\\mathbb R^n$; the affine group is much bigger, but so is the stabiliser of a point, and the quotient comes out the same size. **More symmetry does not mean more room — it means less structure.**\n\nLet us verify the dimension count, and then build $S^2 \\cong SO(3)/SO(2)$ explicitly.",
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "source": "/* Dimensions of the classical groups */\ndim_O(n)   := n*(n-1)/2$        /* antisymmetric matrices */\ndim_E(n)   := dim_O(n) + n$     /* rotation part + translation */\ndim_GL(n)  := n^2$\ndim_Aff(n) := n^2 + n$\ndim_PGL(n) := n^2 - 1$          /* (n×n) matrices modulo scalars */\n\nprint(\"  n    dim E(n)  dim O(n)   difference\")$\nfor n : 1 thru 4 do\n  print(\"  \", n, \"      \", dim_E(n), \"        \", dim_O(n), \"         \", dim_E(n) - dim_O(n))$\nprint(\"→ E(n)/O(n) has dimension n, as ℝⁿ must\")$\n\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"  n    dim Aff(n)  dim GL(n)   difference\")$\nfor n : 1 thru 4 do\n  print(\"  \", n, \"      \", dim_Aff(n), \"         \", dim_GL(n), \"          \", dim_Aff(n) - dim_GL(n))$\nprint(\"→ same quotient dimension, from a much larger group\")$\n\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"  n    dim O(n+1)  dim O(n)   difference     (Sⁿ)\")$\nfor n : 1 thru 4 do\n  print(\"  \", n, \"      \", dim_O(n+1), \"         \", dim_O(n), \"          \", dim_O(n+1) - dim_O(n))$\n\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"  n    dim PGL(n+1)  minus n =  dim of point stabiliser in ℝPⁿ\")$\nfor n : 1 thru 4 do\n  print(\"  \", n, \"      \", dim_PGL(n+1), \"            \", dim_PGL(n+1) - n)$",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": 3,
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "  n    dim E(n)  dim O(n)   difference\n   1        1          0           1\n   2        3          1           2\n   3        6          3           3\n   4        10          6           4\n→ E(n)/O(n) has dimension n, as ℝⁿ must\n  n    dim Aff(n)  dim GL(n)   difference\n   1        2           1            1\n   2        6           4            2\n   3        12           9            3\n   4        20           16            4\n→ same quotient dimension, from a much larger group\n  n    dim O(n+1)  dim O(n)   difference     (Sⁿ)\n   1        1           0            1\n   2        3           1            2\n   3        6           3            3\n   4        10           6            4\n  n    dim PGL(n+1)  minus n =  dim of point stabiliser in ℝPⁿ\n   1        3              2\n   2        8              6\n   3        15              12\n   4        24              20"
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "source": "/* --- S² as SO(3)/SO(2), explicitly --- */\nRz3(θ) := matrix([cos(θ), -sin(θ), 0], [sin(θ), cos(θ), 0], [0, 0, 1])$\nRy3(θ) := matrix([cos(θ), 0, sin(θ)], [0, 1, 0], [-sin(θ), 0, cos(θ)])$\n\nnpole : cvec([0, 0, 1])$\n\n/* Here we want products left as products, so ratsimp — not trigrat, which\n   would expand cos φ sin θ into a sum of angles and obscure the answer. */\nmsimp(e) := matrixmap(ratsimp, e)$\n\n/* A coset representative: swing the north pole to colatitude θ, longitude φ */\ng(θ, φ) := Rz3(φ) . Ry3(θ)$\nprint(\"g(θ,φ) · north pole = \", transpose(msimp( g(θ, φ) . npole )))$\nprint(\"  — spherical coordinates fall straight out of the group action\")$\n\n/* The stabiliser of the north pole is exactly the rotations about the z-axis */\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"Rz(ψ) · pole − pole = \", msimp( Rz3(ψ) . npole - npole ),\n      \"   (fixed for every ψ)\")$\n\n/* Two group elements give the same point iff they differ by such a rotation */\nprint(\"g(θ,φ)Rz(ψ) · pole − g(θ,φ) · pole = \",\n      msimp( g(θ, φ) . Rz3(ψ) . npole - g(θ, φ) . npole ))$\nprint(\"  — so points of S² are cosets g·SO(2):  S² = SO(3)/SO(2)\")$\n\n/* The image always lands on the unit sphere */\nprint(\"\")$\nu : msimp( g(θ, φ) . npole )$\nprint(\"|g(θ,φ)·pole|² = \", trigsimp( u[1][1]^2 + u[2][1]^2 + u[3][1]^2 ))$\nprint(\"and (θ,φ) over [0,π]×[0,2π) covers the whole sphere, so the action\")$\nprint(\"is transitive — which is what makes S² a homogeneous space at all.\")$",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": 6,
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "g(θ,φ) · north pole =\n                      matrix([cos(phi)*sin(theta),sin(phi)*sin(theta),\n                              cos(theta)])\n  — spherical coordinates fall straight out of the group action\nRz(ψ) · pole − pole =  matrix([0],[0],[0])    (fixed for every ψ)\ng(θ,φ)Rz(ψ) · pole − g(θ,φ) · pole =  matrix([0],[0],[0])\n  — so points of S² are cosets g·SO(2):  S² = SO(3)/SO(2)\n|g(θ,φ)·pole|² =  1\nand (θ,φ) over [0,π]×[0,2π) covers the whole sphere, so the action\nis transitive — which is what makes S² a homogeneous space at all."
     ]
    }
