  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "source": "## Summary\n\nKlein's programme, in three statements:\n\n1. **A geometry is a pair $(X, G)$** — a set and a group acting on it. \"Congruent\" means \"in the same $G$-orbit\"; a \"geometric quantity\" means a $G$-invariant function.\n2. **A homogeneous space is a quotient $G/H$**, where $H$ is the stabiliser of a base point. Choosing a base point converts geometry into group theory, and $S^2 = SO(3)/SO(2)$ is the model case.\n3. **Bigger group, fewer invariants.** The subgroup lattice of $PGL(n{+}1)$ is a lattice of geometries, ordered by how much structure each remembers.\n\nThe final computation is the programme in miniature: as the group grew from isometries to projectivities, distance died, then ratio died, and the cross-ratio survived everything.\n\n## What to watch for in the rest of Stage 2\n\nEach remaining notebook is one row of the ladder, taken seriously:\n\n| Notebook | Group | The point |\n|---|---|---|\n| 2.02 | $PGL(3)$ on $\\mathbb{RP}^2$ | points and lines become interchangeable — duality |\n| 2.03 | $PGL(3)$ | the cross-ratio, and why it is *the* projective invariant |\n| 2.04 | $PGL(3)$ | all non-degenerate conics become one conic |\n| 2.05 | $PSL(2,\\mathbb C)$ on $\\hat{\\mathbb C}$ | circles and lines stop being different things |\n| 2.06 | $PSL(2,\\mathbb R)$ on $\\mathbb H^2$ | the parallel postulate fails, and area becomes angle deficit |\n| 2.07 | $O(3)$ on $S^2$ | the same story with the opposite sign of curvature |\n\nAnd a caution worth carrying: the Erlangen picture only describes spaces with enough symmetry to be homogeneous. The three constant-curvature geometries of 2.06–2.07 are the last ones for which it works perfectly. From Stage 3 onwards, surfaces have no symmetry group to speak of, and geometry has to be rebuilt locally — which is Riemann's programme, not Klein's. Stage 5 shows the two were the same idea all along.\n\n## Next\n\n**2.02 — The Projective Plane** builds $\\mathbb{RP}^2$ from homogeneous coordinates, adds the line at infinity to $\\mathbb R^2$, and proves that any two distinct lines meet in exactly one point — no exceptions, no parallel case.\n\n## References\n\n- ★ Lee, N.-H. (2020) *Geometry: from Isometries to Special Relativity*. Undergraduate Texts in Mathematics. Cham: Springer. doi:10.1007/978-3-030-42101-4. Organised around exactly this group-theoretic view.\n- ★ Stillwell, J. (2005) *The Four Pillars of Geometry*. Undergraduate Texts in Mathematics. New York: Springer. doi:10.1007/0-387-29052-4. The fourth pillar is transformation groups; Chapters 7–8 are the Erlangen view at this level.\n- Klein, F. (1893) 'A comparative review of recent researches in geometry', trans. M.W. Haskell, *Bulletin of the New York Mathematical Society*, 2(10), pp. 215–249. The English translation of the 1872 *Erlangen Programm* itself, and still readable.\n- Sharpe, R.W. (1997) *Differential Geometry: Cartan's Generalization of Klein's Erlangen Program*. Graduate Texts in Mathematics 166. New York: Springer. The book that carries the caution at the end of this notebook to its conclusion — how Klein's homogeneous picture and Riemann's local one are reconciled. Relevant again at Stage 5.\n- ★ Lee, J.M. (2013) *Introduction to Smooth Manifolds*. 2nd edn. New York: Springer. Chapter 21 for homogeneous spaces $G/H$ as smooth manifolds.\n\n---\n*Marked ★ = consulted directly while writing; the rest are standard references given from knowledge, not consulted here. Every code cell in this notebook was executed and its output is the real result; the prose is written from knowledge and checked against these sources where they cover the material.*",
   "metadata": {}
  }
 ]
